# Cim (Common Input Method)

Cim is a toolkit-independent input method API for Linux and FreeBSD. It
provides a common C ABI for input method plugins and an Ada-facing API for Ada
applications.

The current tree contains the Cim core, GTK 3 and Qt 6 input method bridges,
and a native libhangul sample plugin.

## Goals

Cim is designed to provide:

- a toolkit-independent input method API;
- a simple plugin architecture;
- low integration cost for input method implementations; and
- stable C ABI boundaries that can be used from different languages.

## Build

The Rake build separates object artifacts by target, profile, and library type,
and library and binary artifacts by target and profile:

```text
build/obj/<target>/<profile>/<library-type>
build/lib/<target>/<profile>
build/bin/<target>/<profile>
```

Cim supports two library types:

- `static-pic` is the default and builds the position-independent static
  `libcim.a`. This is the only supported static form and may be embedded in a
  shared module.
- `relocatable` builds `libcim.so`.

Non-PIC `static` builds are unsupported.

```sh
rake build static-pic
rake build relocatable
rake test static-pic
rake test relocatable
```

Use `TARGET` or `CIM_TARGET` to select the toolchain target triple:

```sh
rake info TARGET=x86_64-linux-gnu
rake cim TARGET=x86_64-linux-musl
rake cim TARGET=x86_64-unknown-freebsd PROFILE=debug
```

Linux libc variants are distinct ABI targets. For example,
`x86_64-linux-gnu` and `x86_64-linux-musl` do not share native build context,
pkg-config results, or probe artifacts.

## Architecture

```text
Application / Toolkit
        |
        v
libcim.a / libcim.so
        |
        v
~/.config/cim/plugin
        |
        v
Cim plugin
```

`~/.config/cim/plugin` is a symbolic link selecting the active plugin. The
`CIM_PLUGIN` environment variable may override that path for testing or a
controlled deployment.

When the first input context is created, Cim loads the selected plugin and
initializes the host attachment. A plugin may create multiple input contexts.
Cim finalizes and unloads the plugin after the last input context is destroyed.

## Plugin ABI

Plugins export a global `CimPlugin` object named `cim_plugin`. The plugin ABI is
defined by `include/cim.h`.

The plugin descriptor and vtable must remain valid while the plugin is loaded.
The vtable `create` and `destroy` callbacks are required. Plugin `init` and
`fini` callbacks are optional and form a balanced attachment lifecycle when
provided.

ABI compatibility follows the public version fields:

- changing `CIM_MAJOR_VERSION` permits an ABI break;
- minor and micro releases preserve ABI compatibility; and
- Cim rejects a plugin whose major version is incompatible.

See `modules/samples/im-libhangul/im-libhangul.c` for the current native sample
plugin.

## Toolkit Bridges

The current tree provides GTK 3 and Qt 6 bridges:

```text
modules/bridges/gtk3
modules/bridges/qt6
```

Typical selection variables are:

```sh
GTK_IM_MODULE="cim"
QT_IM_MODULE="cim"
```

The bridge modules embed `libcim.a` in the default `static-pic` configuration
and keep their embedded Cim runtime private from the surrounding process.

## Testing

Run the consolidated suite with:

```sh
rake test static-pic
rake test relocatable
```

For direct application testing, select a plugin without changing the
`~/.config/cim/plugin` link by setting an absolute plugin path:

```sh
CIM_PLUGIN=/absolute/path/to/im-libhangul.so application
```

